# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in.

# Backend base URL INCLUDING its /api prefix. Server-only: the browser never
# sees it — every request goes to this app's /api, which next.config.js proxies
# to the backend so the backend's HttpOnly session cookie lands on this origin.
BACKEND_API_URL=http://localhost:8001/api

# Session token verification (the middleware gating /admin and /pos).
# Server-only, all of it. Each key verifies only its own algorithm. With both
# keys set, both kinds of token are accepted; with neither, every token is
# rejected.
#
# JWT_PUBLIC_KEY  The backend's RS256 public key, the public half of the key it
#                 signs with (SPKI PEM, "-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----"; real
#                 newlines, "\n" or "\r\n", and wrapping quotes all work). It
#                 can check a token but never mint one, so this is the key this
#                 app is meant to hold. Always RS256.
# JWT_SECRET      The shared HMAC secret; must match the backend's JWT_SECRET
#                 exactly. A SIGNING key: anything that can read it can mint a
#                 token for any user and role. Only needed until the cutover
#                 below is finished.
# JWT_ALGORITHM   Optional, and only for JWT_SECRET: HS256 (the default), HS384
#                 or HS512, matching the backend. Any other value is ignored.
#
# Cutover to RS256 (NEX-54), in this order:
#   1. Add JWT_PUBLIC_KEY here, keep JWT_SECRET, and deploy (env changes only
#      reach new deployments). If the logs show
#      "[auth] JWT_PUBLIC_KEY could not be imported", fix the key before 2.
#      While both keys are set, every cold start warns that HS256 is still
#      accepted.
#   2. Switch the backend to RS256. During the window the backend accepts
#      HS256 tokens only while its JWT_ACCEPT_HS256 is true, and until step 4
#      it can go back to signing HS256 without touching this app. If logins
#      bounce and the logs show "[auth] RS256 verification failed", the key
#      here is not the public half of the backend's signing key.
#   3. Wait out the token lifetime (the backend's JWT_EXPIRES_MINUTES, 8 hours
#      by default), so no HS256 session is still live.
#   4. Remove JWT_SECRET (and JWT_ALGORITHM) here and deploy. HS256 tokens
#      stop being accepted.
#
# "change-me" is a placeholder for local development only. Never deploy it:
# use the backend's real JWT_SECRET, or after cutover no JWT_SECRET at all.
JWT_SECRET=change-me
# JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
# JWT_PUBLIC_KEY="-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\n...\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----"

# Content-Security-Policy mode. Leave unset (report-only) until the logs show
# no [csp-report] lines from real traffic, then set "enforce".
# CSP_MODE=enforce

# Hosts product photos are served from (image optimizer allowlist + CSP img-src).
# Defaults to the R2 public bucket; comma-separate to add a custom domain.
# IMAGE_HOSTS=pub-5092fdb36fa84b649893cd173e4339b7.r2.dev
